cartridge "THE FORBIDDEN CRYPT/COMPUTE!'S GAZETTE/1985"

' THE FORBIDDEN CRYPT
' A ColecoVision game based on "The Forbidden Crypt", COMPUTE!'s Gazette
' February 1985 pp. 52-53, by Ted Reynolds (Commodore 64 BASIC with machine
' language in DATA lines, disk file "crypt/64"; a VIC-20 8K version
' "crypt/vic" with "a" and "b"). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made
' by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from the C64
' BASIC and its machine code, orig/crypt_64.bas, and watched in jsc64),
' rebuilt for the ColecoVision: drawn stone walls, smooth sprites, sound
' effects and a title tune.
'
' The game (as the C64 code plays it): the crypt is a hall with four
' tomb chambers, each with a door on its left and right side. Walk the
' corridors and step into a door to enter its chamber. Inside wait four
' creatures (snakes, spiders, ghouls or bats: one kind per chamber) and
' the chamber's treasure (chest, chalice, shield, urn). The creatures
' close in on the hero, one pixel at a time on both axes, and bump each
' other and the treasure. The button throws a magic bolt the way the hero
' last walked: at most 3 bolts a visit, one in the air at a time; a bolt
' that meets a creature ends it (10 points). Touching the treasure takes
' it (300 points). The door you came in by is sealed behind you: the way
' out is the door on the far side of the chamber, and you come out of
' the tomb on that side. A creature touching the hero costs a life and the
' treasure taken on this visit (minus 300), and he is put back in the
' corridors. A robbed chamber's doors are barred. When all four treasures are
' out the next level starts; every fourth level gives a life and makes
' everything faster. As the article says for the 64, the guardians move
' faster after a companion is eliminated. Liberty: +25% a kill; the C64
' moves one live creature per pass in turn (x4 / creatures left, the last
' one as fast as the hero), which left the bolts useless. 5 lives; no lives left ends the game.
'
' Speeds: the C64 machine code moves the hero one pixel and one of the
' four creatures one pixel per pass of its loop, the bolt two (about 95
' passes a second at level 1 by its delay loop; creatures timed in jsc64
' at about 20 pixels a second). The port's chamber is 208 pixels wide (the
' C64's 304), so: hero 1 px a frame, each creature 0.25, the bolt 2; each
' fourth level all of them about 15% faster; each kill adds 25% to the
' others (0.31, 0.38, 0.44 px at level 1), always slower than the hero (the C64 shortened its delay
' loop), up to level 16.
'
' Liberties: the corridors are walked with a small sprite gliding from
' cell to cell (the C64 moved a ball character); a diagonal into a wall
' slides along it; creatures are never placed within reach of the door
' you enter by, and the treasure never on a creature (the C64 could put a
' creature on the doorstep); a blocked creature tries one axis, then the
' other (the C64 stepped it back); contact is a box of 11 pixels; a robbed
' chamber's door shows bars and a gold mark inside the tomb (the C64 a
' solid block); a caught hero is put back outside the door he came in by
' (the C64 put him outside the right-hand door unless he was caught at the
' very left wall); the chamber number and bolts left are shown on the top
' line; the caught burst lasts 1.5 s; the game ends with a message and
' returns to the title (the C64 asked for a key to play again).
'
' Controls: joystick (either controller) walks; either button throws a
' bolt (held: one every half second). On the title, up and down choose a
' line and a button starts (or shows the credits).
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.

